{"authored" [{"question" "q-82050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\n{R}_{\\theta }=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ are positive, we must have $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians anticlockwise about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "26e0df584745c70f92af019f93522dcca77c209317d53bbfe875515e3d4ea10f" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians clockwise about the origin."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin."] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-82050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin."] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(-1,2),(2,-3).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under a **clockwise** rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(0,\\sqrt{2})$\n- $(3,-1)$\n- $(-5,1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the transformation, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the rotation $R$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are \n\n$$\n(0,\\sqrt{2}),\\,(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "26e0df584745c70f92af019f93522dcca77c209317d53bbfe875515e3d4ea10f" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-11967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/state.json" "file_sha256" "723782c36aa86ba20c0d4e71cbc94924e638cd13b2e7340dce3b5af99627e491" "basis" 1833 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055662/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-8462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the geometrical interpretation of the transformation represented by the matrix $A$ given below? \n\n \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072578/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ea118cc3116902a9570a5568096ce1c598dcda978c888c4c69da173fca05ca3"} {"question" "q-8462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The rotation matrix ${R}_{\\theta }$ represents a rotation of angle $\\theta $ counterclockwise about the origin and is given by\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nComparing $A$ with the rotation matrix, we have \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta <0$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta >0$ we must have $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi .$ \n\nDividing the first equation by the second gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-1.\n$$\n\nThe principal value $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians *clockwise* about the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072578/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ea118cc3116902a9570a5568096ce1c598dcda978c888c4c69da173fca05ca3"} {"question" "q-8462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072578/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bea49cac97e9ba0eeacea89fec567b1759c075ac7b74ca8370ffbb975e7f5996"} {"question" "q-8462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072578/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ea118cc3116902a9570a5568096ce1c598dcda978c888c4c69da173fca05ca3"} {"question" "q-8462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin."] ["text" "A rotation of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin."] ["text" "A rotation of $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin."] ["text" "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians clockwise about the origin."] ["text" "A rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072578/state.json" "file_sha256" "0ea118cc3116902a957